  "query_preview": "..Current satellite shows a cumulus field over\ncentral/eastern Pima County, Santa Cruz County, and parts of \nCochise and Graham Counties with an isolated thunderstorm over \nnorthern Greenlee County and the Santa Ritas. This likely means \nthere is very little CIN left and with more surface heating the \natmosphere will be ready for convection. \n\nAll the ingredients are in place with seasonable PWAT values, \nMLCAPE of 800-1800 J/kg, and effective bulk shear 20-30 kts. \nStorms will continue to develop over the White Mountains early \nthis afternoon and over the Sky Islands by mid-afternoon. Storms\nthen move into the lower elevations and progress westward \nthrough the late afternoon and evening. Storms could reach \nTucson as early as 5 PM, but between 6-8 PM looks more likely. \nStorms will be capable of strong to severe wind gusts with an \nisolated storm or two producing a 70+ mph wind gusts. Model \nsoundings show a dry nose just above 500 mb that could enhance \ndowndrafts and the 05/12Z HREF has a 30 percent chance for 50 kt\nwinds within 40 km of a point for central/eastern Pima County \nand southern Pinal County this evening. This is being driven by \na couple HREF members that shows storms over Tucson that produce\na strong outflow that moves through the metro and then into the\nTohono O'odham Nation and Pinal County late this evening. If \nthis does happen expect blowing dust along I-10 in Pinal County.\nLocally heavy rainfall leading to flash flooding will also be a\nthreat from central Pima County eastward late this afternoon \nand evening. Most storms will be capable of one inch per hour \nrainfall rates with isolated areas approaching 3 inch totals \nthrough midnight. Because of this a Flood Watch has been issued \nfrom 4 PM to 11 PM from central Pima County east to western \nCochise County including the Tucson metro and Santa Cruz County.\n\nOvernight convection looks limited after midnight with maybe a\nisolated showers over central or western Pima County. Thursday\nthunderstorm coverage and intensity looks to be a little less\nthan today. 0-6 km shear values will be around 15-20 kts, but\nthere will be plenty of instability and moisture values will be\nseasonable. CAMs show initial development over the White\nMountains and Sky Islands of Cochise and Graham Counties, then\nspread into the lower elevations mainly south and east of\nTucson. Storms will be capable of strong outflow winds, with a \nfew storms possibly producing severe wind gusts. Storms will \nalso be capable of heavy rainfall with an isolated flash \nflooding threat. \n\nFriday into early next week the high remains in a favorable\nposition for daily afternoon and evening thunderstorms with some\nday to day variation in coverage and strength due to the \nstrength of the high. Friday into Saturday the ridge increases \nin strength which will lead to a 2-3 degree warmup compared to \ntoday and Thursday. This could lead to a downturn in monsoon \nactivity Friday. Saturday ensembles and deterministic models \nshow an increase in mid-level flow, which could lead to an \nincrease in storm coverage and intensity even with the stronger\nridge in place. After Saturday heights decrease and \ntemperatures drop to around normal next week.",
